WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.470
Here’s a puzzle with four

2
00:00:02.470 --> 00:00:04.410
rows and four columns.

3
00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:09.240
Every row, every column and every box must

4
00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:12.660
have the numbers one to four, once each.

5
00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:14.800
Where do you start?

6
00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:19.070
Today: puzzles with rules, and

7
00:00:19.070 --> 00:00:20.810
how rules save guesses.

8
00:00:22.190 --> 00:00:24.050
This puzzle is called a sudoku.

9
00:00:24.710 --> 00:00:27.410
The rule is simple: no number twice

10
00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:29.850
in a row, a column or a box.

11
00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:32.660
Some numbers are given to start you off.

12
00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:36.190
For an empty square, think of

13
00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:37.790
every number that could go there:

14
00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:40.610
one, two, three or four.

15
00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:44.140
Then cross out every number already in

16
00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:46.520
its row, its column and its box.

17
00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:49.250
What’s left are its candidates.

18
00:00:50.630 --> 00:00:53.170
Sometimes only one candidate is left.

19
00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:56.420
Then that square is solved, for sure.

20
00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:59.150
Write it in, and cross it

21
00:00:59.150 --> 00:00:59.850
out of its neighbours.

22
00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:01.730
Quick check!

23
00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:05.610
A square’s row already has one and three.

24
00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:07.700
Its column has four.

25
00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:09.400
What goes in the square?

26
00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:13.010
Two: it’s the only number left.

27
00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:15.860
That’s how rules do the work for you.

28
00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:18.180
On a four by four sudoku,

29
00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:20.520
crossing out solves the whole

30
00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:22.520
puzzle without a single guess.

31
00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:26.710
Big puzzles can get stuck: every empty

32
00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:29.050
square still has two or more candidates.

33
00:01:29.890 --> 00:01:31.890
Then guess in the square with the

34
00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:33.870
fewest, and keep crossing out.

35
00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:36.660
If you reach a dead end, undo the

36
00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:38.200
guess and try the next one.

37
00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:41.860
A computer that only guesses needed

38
00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:44.280
thirteen thousand five hundred and

39
00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:46.680
twenty-eight guesses on a hard nine by

40
00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:47.340
nine sudoku.

41
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:50.500
Crossing out first, it needed four.

42
00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:52.640
That’s why computers that plan

43
00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:55.040
school timetables use rules first.

44
00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:58.970
Timetables have rules just like puzzles:

45
00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:02.370
no teacher in two classes at once.

46
00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:05.050
They can have fair rules too.

47
00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:08.290
For example: nobody always gets

48
00:02:08.290 --> 00:02:09.750
the last lesson of the week.

49
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:12.780
In the app, pick four by

50
00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:14.960
four and press One step.

51
00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:17.850
Watch the small candidates disappear.

52
00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:20.960
Then try a hard nine by nine

53
00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:22.140
and count the guesses.

54
00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:24.240
Your turn.

55
00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:26.900
Every empty square has

56
00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:28.400
two candidates left.

57
00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:30.390
What do you do?

58
00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:34.530
Guess in one square, keep crossing out,

59
00:02:34.850 --> 00:02:37.090
and undo a guess that leads to a dead end.

60
00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:40.870
Remember: rules cross out what can’t be.

61
00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:42.470
Only one left?

62
00:02:43.270 --> 00:02:43.910
Write it in.

63
00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:46.260
And guess only when you’re stuck.

64
00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:50.220
Try it yourself: make a four by four

65
00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:52.580
sudoku for a friend, with a few numbers

66
00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:52.860
given.

67
00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:55.080
Swap puzzles and solve

68
00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:56.500
them by crossing out.

69
00:02:57.150 --> 00:02:58.470
Did anyone need a guess?
